    18	#     - keep the output as the legend-cropped main grid with teal beam trails
    19	#   fused_rule:
    20	#     original_behavior:
    21	#       - all 4 beams are traced independently, so their order is irrelevant
    22	#       - colored obstacle rectangles never change after a hit
    23	#     combo_behavior:
    24	#       - fire beams in the fixed order up -> right -> down -> left
    25	#       - once a beam finishes, every newly painted teal trail cell becomes a blocking obstacle for later beams
    26	#       - when a beam hits a colored obstacle, it turns by the legend and that whole obstacle rectangle disappears
    27	#       - later beams therefore depend on both earlier trail placement and earlier obstacle consumption
    28	#   concrete_small_example:
    29	#     - the up beam first hits the north block and turns left, then hits the west block and heads downward
    30	#     - this consumes the west block and leaves a vertical teal trail near the left side
    31	#     - the right beam later hits the east block, turns downward, then consumes the south block and exits right
    32	#     - under the old task the down beam would still hit the south block and bend, but here that block is gone
    33	#     - under the old task the left beam would travel farther left, but here it stops when it reaches the up beam's trail
    34	#   complexity_calibration:
    35	#     - a fully random generator would often hide one of the two dimensions, so the generator uses a single
    36	#       deliberate 4-obstacle template:
    37	#         * up beam uses north + west
    38	#         * right beam uses east + south
    39	#         * down beam demonstrates south-block erasure
    40	#         * left beam demonstrates blocking by an earlier trail
    41	#     - this keeps the fusion visible in every accepted sample and avoids ambiguous "maybe sequential, maybe not" grids
    42	#     - trail interaction is implemented as blocking instead of inventing a new trail-turn legend, because the
    43	#       combo description only says earlier trails become obstacles. Blocking is the most literal and the cleanest.
    44	#   main_line_assumption_audit:
    45	#     - legend parsing still assumes color 5 is confined to the top band; the generator preserves that
    46	#     - the old solver treated color 8 as non-obstructing paint; that is no longer safe because earlier teal trails
    47	#       now block later beams, so the solver uses a separate earlier_trail_mask
    48	#     - obstacle erasure breaks the old assumption that the main grid is static during tracing, so the solver keeps
    49	#       a mutable obstacle layer distinct from the painted output
